  "injection": "a claim is injected into a question when one of its rules applies to some candidate pair with `same` replaced by `inject_same` (other rule fields kept); claims with inject=false are never injected",
  "claims": [
    {
      "id": "O1",
      "kind": "optimizer",
      "parents": [
        "K0009",
        "K0019",
        "K0022",
        "K0044",
        "K0156",
        "K0198"
      ],
      "text": "For the MLP tasks, among adaptive optimizers (Adam, AdamW, RMSprop, Adagrad) the candidate with the larger cumulative step size reaches lower test loss under these budgets: step size is lr x steps for Adam/AdamW/RMSprop and 2 x lr x sqrt(steps) for Adagrad. This also held when the architectures differed.",
      "inject_same": [],
      "rules": [
        {
          "scope": [
            [
              "family",
              "ne",
              "bigram_lm"
            ],
            [
              "opt",
              "in",
              [
                "Adam",
                "AdamW",
                "RMSprop",
                "Adagrad"
              ]
            ]
          ],
          "predict": {
            "higher_better": "disp"
          }
        }
      ],
      "status": "testable",
      "validity_status": "proposed",
      "dev_evidence": "epoch-9 natural: 0.95 of 559 decisive pairs (MLP optimizer-only 298/301), 25/25 lineages."
    },
    {
      "id": "O2",
      "kind": "optimizer",
      "parents": [
        "K0020",
        "K0100",
        "K0105"
      ],
      "text": "For the bigram language-model tasks (transformer or GRU), the same cumulative-step-size rule holds for learning rates up to 1e-3, but Adam, AdamW and RMSprop at lr 3e-3 are unstable: with 1024 or more steps they lose to any adaptive optimizer at lr 1e-3 or lower, and with 256 steps they are no better than a coin flip.",
